    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2779a28d-7b45-5e35-b56a-20d2750134dd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "To which of the following sets do all the negative rational numbers belong?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We must check whether each set contains all the negative rational numbers.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$ contains the nonpositive real numbers. By the number-set hierarchy and the sign restrictions, every member of the given class belongs to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative rational numbers. For instance, $-1$ is in the given class but not in $\\mathbb{Q}_0^{+}$. Therefore, this set does not contain all the negative rational numbers.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ contains the negative rational numbers. By the number-set hierarchy and the sign restrictions, every member of the given class belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all integers. For instance, $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is in the given class but not in $\\mathbb{Z}$. Therefore, this set does not contain all the negative rational numbers.\n\nTherefore, select $\\mathbb{R}_0^{-}$, $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ only.",
